  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "Represents heaven (天), earth (旦), or the number 1"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "A single horizontal stroke, representing the number one."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "惟初太始，道立於一，造分天地，化成萬物。凡一之屬皆从一。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Ideogram (指事): a horizontal stroke, indicating the number “one”.\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *ʔit. Descendants from the *ʔit etymon account for the majority of the dialectal forms for “one” in Chinese (Mandarin, Jin, Gan, Hakka, Hui, Xiang, Yue, Wu groups, etc.)."
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Form used as the colloquial reading for “one” in most Min varieties: Fuzhou/Fuding suoʔ⁸, Fuqing θyo⁸, Gutian syøʔ⁸, Ningde søʔ⁸, Zhouning sɔʔ⁸, Putian ɬoʔ⁸, Xiamen/Quanzhou/Yongchun/Zhangzhou tsit̚⁸, Leizhou/Haikou ziak⁸, Longyan tse⁴, Youxi ɕie⁷, Jian'ou/Songxi tsi⁵, Jianyang tsi⁸. Whether all of the forms above are related and their origins remain unelucidated; they may be derived from 蜀 (shǔ) (cf. 獨 /独 (dú, “single, alone”); often written when read with the colloquial reading), 隻 /只 (zhī) (< *kV-tek) or 禃 (zhí). Some Fujian dialects use 個 /个 and 寡 (guǎ) for “one”."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "惟初太始，道立於一，造分天地，化成萬物。凡一之屬皆从一。",
